Sentence 2: Hyacinthoides
Hyacinthoides Hyacinthoides is a genus of flowering plants in the family Asparagaceae, known as bluebells.
Sentence 3: "Hyacinthoides" is classified in the subfamily Scilloideae (now part of the family Asparagaceae, but formerly treated as a separate family, called Hyacinthaceae), alongside genera such as "Scilla", "Chionodoxa" and "Ornithogalum".
Sentence 4: "Hyacinthoides" is differentiated from these other genera by the presence of two bracts at the base of each flower, rather than one bract per flower or no bracts in the other genera.
Sentence 5: Hyacinthoides
are distributed around the Mediterranean Basin, with only one species, "Hyacinthoides non-scripta" (the familiar spring flower of bluebell woods in the British Isles and elsewhere) occurring further north
