Sentence 1: Akita (dog)
Akita (dog) The is a large breed of dog originating from the mountainous regions of northern Japan.
Sentence 2: There are two separate varieties of Akita: a Japanese strain, commonly called "Akita Inu" ("inu" means "dog" in Japanese), or "Japanese Akita"; and an American strain, known as the "Akita" or "American Akita".
Sentence 3: The Akita has a short double-coat similar to that of many other northern spitz breeds such as the Siberian Husky, but long-coated
Sentence 4: Akita (dog)
was known in some countries as the "Great Japanese Dog".
Sentence 5: Both forms of Akita are probably best known worldwide from the true story of Hachikō, a loyal Akita who lived in Japan before World War II.
Sentence 6: There is debate among fanciers whether there are two separate breeds of Akita.
Sentence 7: To date, only the American Kennel Club, consider American and Japanese Akitas to be two varieties of the same breed, allowing free breeding between the two.
