Sentence 2: Although the family Acoraceae was originally described in 1820, since then "Acorus" has traditionally been included in Araceae in most classification systems, as in the Cronquist system.
Sentence 3: The family has recently been resurrected as molecular systematic studies have shown that "Acorus" is not closely related to Araceae or any other monocot family, leading plant systematists to place the genus and family in its own order.
Sentence 4: Acorus
Acorus Acorus is a genus of monocot flowering plants.
Sentence 5: This genus was once placed within the family Araceae (aroids), but more recent classifications place it in its own family Acoraceae and order Acorales, of which it is the sole genus of the oldest surviving line of monocots.
Sentence 6: However, modern phylogenetic studies demonstrate that "Acorus" is sister to all other monocots.
