Sentence 1: Amdahl Corporation
Amdahl Corporation Amdahl Corporation was an information technology company which specialized in IBM mainframe-compatible computer products, some of which were regarded as supercomputers competing with those from Cray Research.
Sentence 2: Founded in 1970 by Gene Amdahl, a former IBM computer engineer best known as chief architect of System/360, it has been a wholly owned subsidiary of Fujitsu since 1997.
Sentence 3: From its first machine in 1975, Amdahl's business was to provide mainframe computers that were plug-compatible with contemporary IBM mainframes, but offering higher reliability, running somewhat faster, and costing somewhat less.
Sentence 4: Amdahl's software team developed VM/PE, software designed to optimize the performance of IBM's MVS operating system when running under IBM's VM operating system.
Sentence 5: The corporation went on to distribute an IBM-plug-compatible front-end processor (the 4705) as well as high-performance disk drives, both jointly developed with Fujitsu engineers.
Sentence 6: Bergman began his career at IBM, and later worked in management roles at Texas Instruments, Inc. and Exponential Inc.
Sentence 1: Alemannic German
Alemannic German Alemannic, or rarely Alemmanish (German: ), is a group of dialects of the Upper German branch of the Germanic language family.
Sentence 2: The name derives from the ancient Germanic alliance of tribes known as the Alemanni ("all men").
Sentence 3: In Germany and other European countries, the abstand and ausbau language framework is used to
Sentence 4: High Alemannic German
High Alemannic German High Alemannic is a dialect of Alemannic German spoken in the westernmost Austrian state of Voralberg, on the border with Switzerland and Liechtenstein.
Sentence 5: The High Alemannic dialects are spoken in Liechtenstein and in most of German-speaking Switzerland (Swiss Plateau), except for the Highest Alemannic dialects in the Swiss Alps and for the Low Alemannic (Basel German) dialect in the North West.
Sentence 6: Therefore, High Alemannic must not be confused with the term "Swiss German", which refers to all Alemannic dialects of Switzerland as opposed to Swiss variant of Standard German
