Sentence 1: Snoqualmie people
Snoqualmie people The Snoqualmie people ("S·dukʷalbixʷ") are a southern Coast Salish indigenous people of the Pacific Northwest Coast.
Sentence 2: Snoqualmie is a dialect of the Southern Puget Sound Salish language, which is a Lushootseed language, belonging to the Central Salish language family.
Sentence 3: The commonly called Cowlitz language or "Sƛ̕púlmš" is placed closer to Upper Chehalis language (than Lower Chehalis to Upper Chehalis) and belong to the Tsamosan (Olympic) branch of the Coast Salish family of Salishan languages and was spoken by the "Lower Cowlitz / Cowlitz proper".
Sentence 4: The name Salish refers to groups of indigenous peoples in the Pacific Northwest whose speech and culture share enough of a commonality to suggest an ancient relationship, in a similar vein to the name Indo-Europeans or the name Polynesians.
Sentence 5: The Nuxálk region, farther north, also speak the Salish dialect.
